1. 

Not knowing whom to prioritise or give importance to



What this usually means



This feedback often means:

You treat everyone equally in commitment, time, and energy

But the people closest to you feel they are not getting priority

You may say “yes” to too many people, or avoid saying “no”



A simple framework to prioritise people



Think of your relationships as three circles:



Inner Circle — Highest priority

Family (supportive)

2–3 closest friends

Partner

These people receive:

reliability

time

emotional availability

consistency



Middle Circle — Medium priority

colleagues

acquaintances

extended social group



Outer Circle — Low priority

strangers

casual connections

people who are only transactional



Practical actions

Say “no” more often to outer circle

Be highly consistent with inner circle

If there is a conflict:

Inner circle wins first

Work obligations next

Social / optional people last



Small behaviour changes

Reply sooner to important people

Show up when you commit

Remember key dates / events

Show appreciation



Warning sign to avoid



If everyone says:



“You are reliable for others, but unavailable for us”


Then you need to rebalance priority.

2. 

Not expressing emotions at the right time



What this usually looks like

You feel appreciation or sympathy

But you don’t express it immediately

Others assume you do not care

Later when you explain, it feels “too late”



This is very common for logical thinkers.



Why this happens

You process emotions internally

You avoid vulnerability

You think actions matter more than words



But, for most people, emotional acknowledgment in real-time matters.



Skills to practice



A. Acknowledge emotions immediately — even briefly

Examples:



Good moment

“Thanks, that really meant a lot.”

“I’m happy you shared this with me.”



Hard moment

“I’m really sorry this happened to you.”

“That sounds difficult — I’m here for you.”



B. Don’t overthink wording

Emotion matters more than grammar.



C. Use short statements

If full discussion feels hard, start with:

“I appreciate you.”

“I understand.”

“I care.”



D. Practice noticing moments

Ask yourself:

Did someone do something kind?

Did someone share something painful?



If yes → respond emotionally first

Logic can come later.

3. 

Responding logically instead of emotionally (especially with women)



This is extremely common among analytical people.



What they expect first



Emotional validation



Not solutions.

Not reasoning.

Not debate.



Example difference



She says:



“I had a terrible day. My boss was rude.”


Logical answer (not helpful):

“Maybe he was stressed.”

“You should ignore it.”

“Just focus on your work.”



Emotionally supportive answer (better):

“I’m sorry that happened. That must have felt awful.”

“I understand why you feel upset.”

“You didn’t deserve that.”



Golden rule



First: Emotional support

Later: Logical discussion



Think order:

Connect

Comfort

Support

Solve (only if asked)



Sentences you can use

“I understand why you’d feel that way.”

“That sounds really tough.”

“I’m here with you.”

“Tell me more.”



Avoid

Judging

Explaining immediately

Comparing to your story

Minimizing feelings (“It’s not a big deal”)

How to Practically Train These Skills (Daily Habits)



Habit 1 — Pause 3 seconds before responding



Ask:

What emotion are they feeling?

What do they need from me right now?



Habit 2 — Label the emotion



Examples:

“You sound disappointed.”

“You seem stressed.”

“You look really happy.”



This creates connection.



Habit 3 — Express appreciation out loud



Daily goals:

Say thank you sincerely

Praise effort

Recognise support



Habit 4 — Journal reflection (5 minutes)



Answer:

Did I validate someone today?

Did I ignore emotions?

Did I prioritise the right people?

What Will Improve Over Time

Relationships feel warmer

People will trust you more

You will appear emotionally mature

Women will feel safe opening up to you

Your inner circle will feel valued

Important Point



None of this means you must stop being logical or practical.



You are simply adding emotional intelligence on top of logic.



Think of it as:

Logic = problem solving

Emotion = connection building



Healthy relationships need both.

